Instead of pushing only nodes, this version stores a small state with each node. State 0 means the node still needs its left side and right side arranged. State 1 means its left side is finished, so its value can be added.

Pushing the right node first and the left node last makes the left side come out of the stack first.

```csharp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     public int data;
 *     public TreeNode left;
 *     public TreeNode right;
 *     public TreeNode(int val) {
 *         this.data = val;
 *         this.left = null;
 *         this.right = null;
 *     }
 * }
 */

public class Solution {
    public List<int> Inorder(TreeNode root) {
        List<int> result = new List<int>();
        if (root == null) {
            return result;
        }

        Stack<(TreeNode node, int state)> stack = new Stack<(TreeNode, int)>();
        stack.Push((root, 0));

        while (stack.Count > 0) {
            var item = stack.Pop();
            TreeNode node = item.node;

            if (item.state == 1) {
                result.Add(node.data);
                continue;
            }

            if (node.right != null) {
                stack.Push((node.right, 0));
            }
            stack.Push((node, 1));
            if (node.left != null) {
                stack.Push((node.left, 0));
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

The time complexity is O(n). The stack and the stored states use O(n) space in the worst case. The empty tree and a single leaf are returned as `[]` and `[value]` respectively.

The order is left subtree, current node, right subtree. The helper follows exactly that order and keeps adding values to one result vector. A null node stops the recursion.

For a tree with root 1, left child 2, and right child 3, the left side is completed before 1 is added.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution{
    void visit(TreeNode* node, vector<int>& result) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return;
        }

        visit(node->left, result);
        result.push_back(node->data);
        visit(node->right, result);
    }

public:
    vector<int> inorder(TreeNode* root){
        vector<int> result;
        visit(root, result);
        return result;
    }
};
```

The time cost is O(n), since every node is visited once. The recursion uses O(h) space, where h is the tree height, and the returned vector stores O(n) values.

A stack can copy the part of recursion that remembers parent nodes. The code first pushes every node on the left path. After reaching null, it removes the last saved node, records it, and continues with its right child.

This also handles an empty tree because the loop never starts when both the root and stack are empty.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> inorder(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        TreeNode current = root;

        while (current != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {
            while (current != null) {
                stack.push(current);
                current = current.left;
            }

            current = stack.pop();
            result.add(current.data);
            current = current.right;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(n). The stack holds at most O(h) nodes, so the extra space is O(h), not counting the result list.

